## Coordination and ops

| Trigger phrase | MCP call | Output |
|---|---|---|
| `/oxagen:claim <file:line \| symbol>`, "claim X" | `agent.claim_work({ scope })` | Claim id + expiry. |
| `/oxagen:incident open\|resolve\|link`, "open / resolve / link incident" | `incident.open` / `incident.resolve` / `incident.link` | Incident id + affected nodes. |
| `/oxagen:workspace-select [name]`, "switch workspace" | `workspace.list()` → prompt → `workspace.bind({ id })` | New workspace id + confirmation. |
| `/oxagen:go [--p0 \| --issue OXA-N]`, "dispatch the swarm" | Dispatch is a server-side action — direct the user to `https://app.oxagen.ai/swarm/dispatch` with the issue prefilled. | Link + status-poll instructions. |
| `/oxagen:watch [--daemons]`, "start the monitor" | Cursor cannot spawn long-lived daemons inside the editor — direct the user to run `oxagen watch` from a terminal. | Command + docs link. |

## Output conventions (shared across commands)

- Cite `file:line` for every node — the graph stores it.
- Sort caller / dependency lists by PageRank when result count > 5.
- Long lists: show top-10 + `(N more)` footer; do not paginate inside chat.
- Quote actual node names from the graph, not paraphrases.
- Cite memory hits explicitly: `Memory recall (procedure <id>, confidence 0.82): …`.
- On low confidence (`< 0.4`), add a one-line caveat before the result.
- On MCP error: surface the error verbatim, then suggest `/oxagen:setup` to re-verify wiring.

## Failure modes

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| Tool not found / 401 | `OXAGEN_MCP_TOKEN` missing or expired — re-run setup. |
| Empty result for a symbol that exists | Ingest hasn't run — direct the user to **Settings → Connections** in the app and trigger re-ingest, or call `ontology.refresh_repo`. |
| Stale claim blocking another session | Call `agent.list_active_claims`, identify the claim, override with `agent.claim_work({ scope, force: true })` — records an audit event. |
| Low-confidence answers from `ontology.ask` | Graph may be sparse; recommend re-ingest and offer to fall back to `code.find_pattern` for the same query. |
